   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the general city law, in relation to exempting the city of\nMiddletown from application of certain provisions of law relating to licensing\nof plumbers",
    "basePrintNo": "S8197",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8197-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §57, Gen City L",
    "lawSection": "General City Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8197\n\nSPONSOR: BONACIC\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the general city law, in relation to\nexempting the city of Middletown from application of certain provisions\nof law relating to licensing of plumbers\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nExempts the City of Middletown from article four of the General City\nLaw, which requires that a city maintain a board of plumbing examiners\nthat is charged with the licensing of master plumbers authorized to do\nbusiness in the city.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1:Amends subdivision 1 of section 57 of the General City Law to\nexempt the City of Middletown from article four of the General City Law,\nwhich requires that a city maintain a board of plumbing examiners that\nis charged with the licensing of master plumbers authorized to do busi-\nness in the city.\n\nSection 2:Effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis legislation was requested by the Mayor of the City of Middletown,\nCounty of Orange.\n\nArticle four of the General City Law requires that cities in New York\nState maintain a board of plumbing examiners. This board is responsible\nfor the licensing of master plumbers who are authorized to do business\nin the city.\n\nThe City of Middletown is having difficulty filling the position of\nplumbing inspector as the applicant pool is extremely limited and there\nare significant issues finding enough plumbers for projects that are\ncurrently under consideration, leading to very important projects being\ndelayed due to the lack of available plumbers.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to State.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the thirtieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law.\n",
